From d3a481bc8d52da9ec5316ae8d675a3c9d7c7ada7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jayneelatintel <jayneel.vora@intel.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:15:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] Vulkan: rework the Intel single-token Q8_0 flash
 attention

The time of a workgroup was a chain of memory trips and barriers, and
its 32 KB of shared memory kept part of the workgroups of a 2K context
from being resident at once, so the dispatch took two rounds.

- scores: lane h of a subgroup is query head h; the subgroup's K rows
  are read with consecutive lanes on consecutive 8-byte pieces, first
  thing in the kernel; Q (8-bit, realigned to the dwords of a Q8_0 row,
  zeros against the scales) stays in registers and K dwords come from
  the lane holding them, so the dot products read no shared memory
- max and sum per head: one exchange of the subgroups' values each, two
  barriers instead of twelve
- P x V: P read once per 8 positions per lane and passed between lanes;
  8 positions of V loads in flight
- the four streams are added up through the P buffer, so shared memory
  is 11.5 KB (CH 128) and all workgroups are resident

test-backend-ops FLASH_ATTN_EXT hsk=hsv=128 passes at chunks 128, 64
and 32. Same pipeline, push constants and spec constants as before.
