-// heads per KV head (Qwen3-235B-A22B and models of the same attention shape).
+// heads per KV head (Qwen3-235B-A22B and models of the same attention shape). Needs subgroups of 16.
 //
-// The generic scalar shader either gives each query head its own workgroup, so every K and V row
-// is loaded and dequantized 16 times, or, with the heads grouped, needs more registers than the
-// Intel compiler has and spills. Here one workgroup takes CH consecutive KV positions of one KV
-// head for all 16 of its query heads, in three steps that share nothing but shared memory:
-//   1. scores: each thread dots one K row against HPT query heads with the int8 dot product
-//      (Q is quantized to 8 bits per 32 values on entry, as the generic MMQ path does);
-//   2. the max of each head over the chunk (a tree in shared memory), then the exponentials;
+// One workgroup takes CH consecutive KV positions of one KV head for all 16 of its query heads.
+// The time of a workgroup is a chain of trips to memory and barriers, so the steps are laid out to
+// keep that chain short and shared memory small (all workgroups of a 2K context resident at once):
+//   1. scores: subgroup k takes positions k*PS .. k*PS+PS-1, lane h query head h. The subgroup's K
+//      rows are read with consecutive lanes on consecutive 8-byte pieces, issued first thing; each lane
+//      keeps its head's Q (8-bit, realigned to the dwords of a Q8_0 row) in registers and takes the K
+//      dwords from the lane that holds them, so the dot products need no shared memory;
+//   2. the max and the sum of each head: one exchange of the subgroups' values each, two barriers;
 //   3. P x V: four streams each walk a quarter of the chunk, a thread owning 4 values of the head
-//      dimension for all 16 heads; the streams are added up in shared memory.
+//      dimension for all 16 heads; P comes from shared memory U positions at a time, one read per lane,
+//      and is passed between lanes; the streams are added up through the same shared memory.
 // The result is always the split-k partial (unnormalized O, row sum L, row max M) that
 // fa_split_k_reduce combines, so sinks and the division by L happen there.
 //
 // A Q8_0 row of 128 values is 136 bytes: blocks (2-byte scale + 32 int8) at bytes 0, 34, 68, 102.
-// K rows are read as 8-byte pairs and V as dwords (the host checks the alignment): each pair of blocks is 17 dwords,
-// the even block's scale is the low half of dword 0 and its quants start mid-dword, the odd
-// block's scale is the high half of dword 8 and its quants are dwords 9..16.
+// K rows are read as 8-byte pieces and V as dwords (the host checks the alignment): each pair of
+// blocks is 17 dwords, the even block's scale is the low half of dword 0 and its quants start
+// mid-dword, the odd block's scale is the high half of dword 8 and its quants are dwords 9..16.
+// The dot product of the even block runs over dwords 0..8 against Q shifted by two bytes, with zeros
+// where the scales are.
 
